# Airspy R2 and Airspy Mini, for sdroxide's native USB driver.
#
# A *different receiver* from the Airspy HF+, which has its own rule file:
# different silicon, different USB id, different protocol. Both may be installed
# together and neither covers the other.
#
# sdroxide talks to these directly with nusb rather than through libusb,
# libairspy or libSoapySDR, but the kernel-level requirement is the same: the
# invoking user needs write access to the usbfs node.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" hands an ACL to whoever is logged in at the active seat, which
# needs no group to exist and no re-login. It is the better half of this rule
# and it is not the whole of it: uaccess grants nothing where there is no seat
# to be logged in at — WSL2, a headless box reached over ssh, a container, or
# sdroxide running as a systemd service — and the device stays root-owned and
# unreachable for reasons nothing on screen explains (issue #350).
#
# So GROUP="plugdev" comes along as a fallback, taking effect for anyone in
# that group when the ACL does not. Where the group does not exist, udev logs
# one line and applies the rest of the rule, so nothing is lost by asking. To
# use it:
#
#   sudo groupadd -f plugdev && sudo usermod -aG plugdev $USER
#
# then log out and back in (`newgrp plugdev` for the current shell).
#
# Install:
#   sudo cp 60-sdroxide-airspy.rules /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload
# then unplug and replug the receiver.
#
# Airspy's own 52-airspy.rules covers the same id. If it is already installed
# this file is harmless duplication, not a conflict.

# One id for both models. Nothing in the USB descriptors distinguishes an R2
# from a Mini — not even the product string — so a rule per model is neither
# possible nor needed.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTRS{idProduct}=="60a1", MODE="0660", GROUP="plugdev", TAG+="uaccess"
